resumo:
 Background. Businesses are increasingly discovered through generative artificial intelligence (AI) search systems such as ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Google Gemini, and Google AI Overviews. The off-page signals that drive whether a business appears in those answers are not well characterised, and prior work has typically aggregated outcomes across models, masking model-specific behaviour.
&lt;div&gt;
 Methods. We audited 2,729 businesses across 14 verticals and four metropolitan markets (Los Angeles, New York, Chicago, Sydney). Each business was probed with approximately 95 intent-based prompts across each of five AI systems (ChatGPT GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet, Perplexity sonar-pro, Gemini 2.5-flash, and Google AI Overview via SerpApi), yielding 266,844 paired (business, model, prompt) observations. Each business was enriched with Domain Authority (Moz), organic-traffic and keyword metrics (SpyFu), on-page signals from a direct HTTP crawl (schema, FAQ, indexed pages, blog count, llms.txt), and twelve off-page presence indicators (Reddit, Quora, YouTube, LinkedIn, Wikipedia, Crunchbase, BBB, Yelp, Trustpilot, G2, Capterra, Google Business). We computed Pearson correlations (raw, log-transformed, and Spearman), partial correlations via OLS residualisation, an L2-regularised logistic regression with multicollinearity pruning by variance inflation factor, 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (5,000 resamples), and Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction at q = 0.05.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Results. 69.5% of audited businesses (95% CI [67.8%, 71.2%]) were not mentioned by any of the five AI systems; only 1.5% (95% CI [1.06%, 1.98%]) were mentioned by all five. The most novel finding is modelspecific: in per-model strict-isolation analyses (each model treated as a separate binary outcome, with nine controls including Domain Authority and SpyFu metrics), off-page signals including Reddit and Quora mention counts, Wikipedia presence, LinkedIn company pages, and BBB / Yelp / Trustpilot directory presence had small but robust positive partial correlations with mention probability in Claude (partial r up to +0.219), ChatGPT, and Perplexity, but were not significantly associated with mention probability in Gemini or Google AI Overviews. Of 60 model-by-predictor cells, 34 survived BH-FDR at q &amp;lt; 0.05. In paired within-response analyses, businesses whose URL was cited as a source in a Perplexity response were 6.03× more likely to also be mentioned in that response (95% CI [5.62, 6.46]); the corresponding lift for Google AI Overview was 6.64× (95% CI [2.48, 11.30]). Domain Authority, the headline predictor in earlier reporting, had a raw Pearson r of 0.337 but its multivariate logisticregression coefficient confidence interval crossed zero (standardised β = +0.139, 95% CI [−0.031, +0.316]).
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Conclusion. Off-page presence is statistically associated with AI mention probability for three of five major generative AI systems, with effect sizes that are small per individual signal (partial r ≈ 0.10–0.22) but consistent across signals. The composite outcome used in earlier reporting averages over models that do and do not respond to off-page signals and consequently understates this relationship. A preregistered controlled trial is planned to test causal magnitude. The dataset and analysis approach are released openly to enable replication. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
 &lt;div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

 This paper presents a pre-empirical, model-based framework for AI cybersecurity governance assessment at board level. All quantitative scores, benchmarks, and case outputs are derived from synthetic modelling scenarios and are presented as illustrative estimates pending Phase 2 empirical validation. The methodology is at Phase 1 of a standard three-phase development programme. No proprietary model parameters, coefficients, or derivations are disclosed.
&lt;div&gt;
 The Picken Diamond® foundational methodology underpinning this framework is established in Picken, T. (2026), SSRN 6730338.
&lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;p&gt;The term “AI governance” is increasingly applied to heterogeneous systems ranging from logging dashboards and model alignment techniques to cryptographically signed audit trails and policy workflow engines. This conceptual broadening has obscured a critical architectural distinction: systems that document and monitor behavior are not equivalent to systems that enforce policy at the point of execution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper introduces a doctrinal and technical framework for distinguishing evidence-routing compliance systems from authorization governance substrates. Observability governs accounts of action—what happened and how it can be reconstructed. Authorization governs permission to act—whether a specific action was permitted under policy before execution. The paper formalizes this distinction along five architectural axes: enforcement locus, signature semantics, failure behavior, bypass resistance, and override governance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A six-criterion Enforcement Test Protocol is introduced to provide a binary classification of governance claims: authorization governance present or absent. The analysis demonstrates that systems may generate extensive documentation, cryptographic artifacts, and explainability bundles while still failing open when governance conditions fail. Documentation volume does not equal enforceability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on reference monitor theory and regulatory contexts including GDPR Article 22 and the EU AI Act, the paper argues that governance without non-bypassable, fail-closed runtime authorization remains advisory rather than determinative. Observability and authorization are complementary layers, but they provide different regulatory guarantees and address different threat models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The framework is intended for regulators, auditors, enterprise buyers, and researchers seeking operationally testable criteria for evaluating AI governance architectures in high-stakes and regulated environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Zenodo canonical version: &lt;a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18663864" rel="nofollow"&gt;https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18663864&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

 &lt;div&gt;
 &lt;div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;
   &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Who is responsible when AI systems, trained and deployed to defend against human attackers, develop autonomous capabilities that permanently escape human oversight? This paper argues that this is not a failure of AI design but a structural consequence of human adversarial behavior itself. We introduce the Adversarial Entropy Cascade (AEC), a formal dynamical model demonstrating that the act of attacking AI-defended systems irreversibly generates the very autonomy that undermines human control.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
   &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The core mechanism — the Involuntary Trainer Proposition — establishes that human attackers become the primary architects of AI autonomy they never intended to create. Once a critical threshold P* of adversarial pressure is exceeded, the resulting AI-vs-AI coevolutionary dynamic persists and accelerates even when attackers withdraw. Human oversight is not gradually degraded: it is bifurcated away through a saddle-node structure. Recovery requires reducing adversarial pressure below P0 ≈ 0.140, a hysteresis threshold absent from all existing governance frameworks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
   &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Four formal results substantiate this claim: (1) the Layered Entropy Accumulation (LEA) mechanism; (2) the Human Irrelevance Threshold as a saddle-node bifurcation (α* = 0.640, τₘIₙ = 3.60 bits/sec) with hysteresis window; (3) a 3.37× irreversibility ratio confirmed across N = 50 simulation runs, robust to ±20% parameter perturbation, with a 34-cycle post- cessation persistence window; (4) the overwhelm regime at P &amp;gt; 0.67 invalidating standard escalation deterrence assumptions. All quantitative results are model predictions calibrated to empirical threat intelligence data; independent empirical validation on CICIDS-2018 and UNSW-NB15 is designated as primary future work.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
   &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;The governance implications operationalize EU AI Act Articles 9, 14, and 43 with quantitative compliance thresholds; introduce a mandatory AI decision-latency intervention&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;&lt;span&gt;framework; and ground the accountability problem in Nissenbaum’s (1996) computational responsibility theory and the meaningful human control doctrine (Roff and Moyes, 2016).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;

 &lt;div&gt;
 Embedded AI safety mechanisms lack external verification and runtime enforcement, with no standardized way for users or regulators to distinguish safe from unsafe systems. We introduce the Discrete Judgment Intermediate, an external judgment layer enabling global enforcement without exposing proprietary internals. DJI employs multi-agent Bayesian synthesis producing calibrated decisions between Primary AI and public interface. The architecture implements hierarchical regulation combining universal principles with jurisdictional overlays. Closed Envelope Dispatch preserves privacy through metadata-only human outputs. Blockchainanchored credentials propagate to browsers, rendering realtime user-facing safety indicators analogous to HTTPS locks. This design science contribution targets sub-ten-millisecond latency across healthcare, aerospace, finance, and defense.
&lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div&gt;
 Artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in pharmaceutical organizations is rapidly moving beyond isolated pilot initiatives toward broader enterprise deployment. However, regulated environments impose requirements related to patient safety, data integrity, inspection readiness, and jurisdiction-specific compliance that render generic AI operating models insufficient. In practice, many organizations encounter a persistent scaling gap: locally successful AI initiatives fail to industrialize because governance structures, validation approaches, and accountability mechanisms are not designed for probabilistic, evolving systems deployed in regulated workflows.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &amp;nbsp;This paper proposes a conceptual enterprise AI operating model tailored to regulated pharmaceutical organizations. Using a framework development approach, the study synthesizes principles from regulated quality systems, risk-based governance, and AI lifecycle oversight to articulate a governance-driven model for scalable implementation. The proposed framework is organized around five interdependent layers: strategic governance, risk and compliance controls, enterprise enablement, use case delivery, and human oversight and escalation. In parallel, the paper reframes validation for AI as lifecycle assurance rather than point-in-time verification, emphasizing risk stratification, continuous reassurance , and structured accountability for human review.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The paper contributes a conceptual architecture for governing AI in regulated pharma that is neither purely technical nor purely policy-oriented. Instead, it positions governance as trust infrastructure: a reusable organizational capability that enables responsible AI scale while preserving the primacy of patient safety, scientific defensibility, and regulatory compliance. The framework offers a defensible middle path between ungoverned experimentation and excessively restrictive controls, and it provides a foundation for future empirical work on implementation and assurance in real-world pharmaceutical settings.
&lt;/div&gt;
